          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 2000 watt generator run an RV air conditioner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not on its own, because the compressor's starting surge exceeds what a typical 2,000-watt inverter can supply. Exceptions are generators built for it, such as Yamaha's EF2400iSHC, which Yamaha says starts most 13,500 BTU units, or a standard 2,000-watt unit paired with a soft-start device."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the quietest RV generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Among our picks, the Westinghouse iGen4500 has one of the lowest listed ratings, at 52 dBA, and the Yamaha EF2400iSHC is listed at 53 to 60 dBA. Ratings are measured at light loads and set distances, so every generator is louder when running an air conditioner. Placement and distance matter as much as the rating."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a dual fuel generator good for an RV?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for many RVers. You already carry propane, it stores without going stale, and it means carrying one fuel instead of two. The trade-off is slightly less power on propane; Champion lists its 4500 dual fuel at about 3,150 running watts on propane against 3,500 on gasoline."
          }
        },
