    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most 30-amp travel trailers, the Westinghouse iGen4500 is the RV generator to buy: enough running watts for a 13,500 BTU air conditioner plus the fridge and lights, a built-in TT-30R outlet, and a low listed noise rating. If you have a 50-amp rig and want one machine, the Champion 8500-watt dual-fuel inverter is the pick. The rest of this guide is about sizing for the air conditioner, which decides everything else.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            An RV generator has a narrower job than a home backup generator. It has to start and run a rooftop air conditioner, it has to be quiet enough for a campground, and it has to connect to the RV's shore power cord without improvised wiring. A generator that fails any one of those is a poor RV generator however many watts it makes.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specification sheets and retailer listings from Westinghouse, Champion, Honda, Yamaha's distributor Koshin, Cummins, Camping World, Tractor Supply and Lowe's. We did not run these generators ourselves. We compared them on running watts, the RV outlet they carry, listed noise, fuel options, runtime and weight, and we say where sources disagree.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the picks are inverter generators. An inverter produces cleaner power for an RV's electronics, throttles down at light loads to save fuel and cut noise, and is quieter than an open-frame generator of the same size. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generator</a> guide covers the category more broadly, including home backup use.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not include open-frame contractor generators, even though some carry a 50-amp RV outlet. They are usually too loud for campgrounds with noise limits, and the money saved tends to be spent again on a quieter generator after the first complaint from a neighboring site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 30-Amp vs 50-Amp RVs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the plug on your RV's shore power cord. A 30-amp RV uses a three-prong TT-30 plug and a single 120-volt leg, so its whole electrical system can draw at most 3,600 watts. A 50-amp RV uses a four-prong 14-50 plug with two 120-volt legs of 50 amps each, for up to 12,000 watts. Those numbers set the ceiling on what a generator ever needs to supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a 30-amp trailer, a generator with around 3,000 to 3,700 running watts and a TT-30R outlet covers nearly everything, including one rooftop air conditioner, as long as you do not run the microwave and the air conditioner at the same moment. Generators around 2,000 rated watts can work with careful load management or a soft-start device, which is where the Yamaha EF2400iSHC and Honda EU3200i fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 50-amp coach with two air conditioners needs much more. Two rooftop units running together, plus a refrigerator and a water heater element, can push past 6,000 watts. The realistic choices are a large inverter with a 50-amp outlet, such as the Champion 8500, or two inverters linked by a parallel kit with a 50-amp outlet on the kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can plug a 50-amp RV into a 30-amp generator with a dogbone adapter, and many owners do. It works, but the RV will only have about 3,600 watts available, so you can run one air conditioner at most. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical running and starting watts if you want to add up your own loads.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
