        <div id="air-conditioner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Starting the Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rooftop air conditioner is the load that decides which generator you need. A typical 13,500 BTU unit draws somewhere around 1,300 to 1,700 watts while running, but the compressor needs a short surge several times higher to start. That starting surge, not the running load, is what trips small generators and makes owners think the generator is faulty.
          </p>

          <p>
            A soft-start device, fitted to the air conditioner, ramps the compressor up gradually and cuts that surge sharply. With one fitted, a generator around 2,000 to 2,800 rated watts can often start a 13,500 BTU unit that it otherwise could not. Fitting a soft start is usually a cheaper fix than buying a bigger generator, and it also reduces the strain on campground pedestals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without a soft start, plan on a generator with roughly 3,000 running watts or more for one 13,500 BTU unit, which is why the Westinghouse iGen4500 and the Champion 4500 dual fuel sit at the top of this list for 30-amp trailers. Yamaha's EF2400iSHC is the exception, because Yamaha designed it specifically to start most 13,500 BTU units despite its 2,000-watt rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manage loads in the order they start. Let the air conditioner start and settle before turning on the microwave, coffee maker or hair dryer, and set the refrigerator to propane if it can run on both. On a 50-amp coach, start the two air conditioners a minute or so apart. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">what size generator you need</a> explains starting and running watts in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Noise and Campground Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise limits are the rule RVers run into most often. National parks are a common example: federal rules for the National Park Service set a limit of 60 decibels measured at 50 feet for generators and other motorized equipment, and individual parks and campgrounds set their own generator hours. Private campgrounds often have stricter rules, and some ban generators entirely in certain loops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers' decibel ratings help, but they are not directly comparable. Most portable generators are rated at 23 feet, often at a light or quarter load, while some built-in sets are listed at 10 feet. A generator listed at 52 dBA at a quarter load will be louder when it is running an air conditioner. Use the ratings to compare similar machines, not as a promise of what a neighbor will hear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement matters as much as the machine. Point the exhaust away from your site and your neighbors, keep the generator on the ground rather than in a pickup bed where the bed amplifies noise, and use a longer cord to get it farther from the camper. Never box a generator in to quiet it; it needs airflow to cool and to keep exhaust from building up.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you boondock where there are no neighbors, noise matters less than fuel and weight. If you camp mostly in developed campgrounds, put noise near the top of your list, and check each campground's generator hours before you arrive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Parallel Kits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many inverter generators can be linked to a second identical or compatible unit with a parallel kit, which combines their output. Two smaller inverters can then supply what one large generator would, and each one is light enough to lift on its own. Champion's 4500-watt dual fuel accepts a parallel kit, which Champion sells with a 50-amp RV outlet on some versions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paralleling has real advantages for RVers. You can take one generator on short trips and both on hot-weather trips, and if one needs service, you still have the other. Two machines at light load can also be quieter than one large machine working harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs are a second generator, a kit, and two engines to maintain. Parallel kits generally only work between specific models, usually the same brand and series, so check the manufacturer's compatibility list before buying a second unit. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-inverter-generator/">best dual fuel inverter generator</a> guide notes which models parallel.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own one 2,000- to 3,500-watt inverter, adding a matching second unit and a kit is often the cheapest route to running a second air conditioner. If you are starting from scratch with a 50-amp coach, a single large inverter like the Champion 8500 is simpler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gas, Propane and Runtime
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most RVs already carry propane for cooking and heating, which makes a dual-fuel generator appealing: one fuel to buy and store, and propane does not go stale or gum up a carburetor the way stored gasoline can. Champion lists its 4500 dual fuel at about 3,150 running watts on propane against 3,500 on gasoline, which illustrates the usual trade: propane delivers somewhat less power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime figures are always quoted at a light load, usually 25 percent. Running an air conditioner pushes a generator well above that, and runtime falls sharply. Honda lists the EU3200i at 8.6 hours at a quarter load but 3.3 hours at its rated load; plan your refueling around the heavier figure on hot days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carry fuel safely. Store gasoline in approved containers outside the living area, and do not refuel a running or hot generator. If you run on propane from the RV's own tanks, make sure the connection is designed for it, and remember that running a generator and the furnace from the same tanks uses propane faster than you might expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            For long stays at one site, a larger fuel tank or a propane connection to a big external tank can matter more than raw output. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-can-a-generator-run-continuously/">how long a generator can run continuously</a> covers runtime and maintenance intervals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="emissions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Emissions, Spark Arrestors and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every generator is sold in every state. California's air resources board sets its own rules for small engines, and some models or versions are listed as not for sale there; one retailer lists that restriction on a Champion 4500 dual fuel version. If you live in or travel through California, check the specific model's status before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many public lands, including national forests, require a spark arrestor on generators to reduce fire risk. Most inverter generators sold for RV use include one, but check before camping on public land in dry seasons, and keep the screen clean, because a clogged arrestor also restricts the engine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide is the serious hazard. Run the generator outdoors only, with the exhaust pointed away from your RV and your neighbors' windows, vents and awnings. Never run it inside a toy hauler garage, under an awning or in an enclosed space. Keep the RV's carbon monoxide alarm working and test it at the start of every trip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some newer generators include carbon monoxide shutoff, such as Champion's CO Shield on the 201319 and Honda's CO shutdown feature on current EU3200i listings. These are worthwhile, but they are a backstop. Placement and a working alarm are still what keeps people safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portable-built-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Portable vs Built-In
          </h2>

          <p>
            Motorhomes and some toy haulers have a generator bay, and a built-in set like the Cummins Onan QG 2800i starts from a switch inside, draws from the coach fuel tank and never needs setting up. For owners who move often and run a generator daily, that convenience is worth a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable inverters cost less, can be moved away from the RV to reduce noise, and can come into the house during a power outage. That last point matters to many owners: a 3,500- to 4,500-watt inverter that runs a trailer in summer can also keep a refrigerator and lights going at home. Our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generator</a> guide covers home backup options, and if you ever want to connect one to the house properly, see our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switch</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacing an existing built-in generator is usually best done with a like-for-like or manufacturer-approved replacement, installed by an RV service center, because the bay, exhaust routing, fuel line and wiring were designed around a specific unit. Fitting a built-in where none existed is a larger job.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your RV has a 30-amp service and no generator bay, a portable inverter is almost always the better buy. If your coach has a bay and a 50-amp service, compare the built-in options your manufacturer approves against a large portable, and weigh the convenience against the cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing for Running Watts Only</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An air conditioner's compressor needs a starting surge several times its running draw. A generator that covers the running load on paper can still trip when the compressor starts. Size for the start, or fit a soft-start device to the air conditioner so a smaller generator can manage it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a 50-Amp Outlet Delivers 50 Amps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some inverters carry a 14-50R outlet rated well below 50 amps; listings rate Champion's 8500-watt unit's outlet at 29.2 amps. It plugs straight into a 50-amp RV, but you still cannot run everything at once. Read the outlet's rating, not just its shape.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Decibel Ratings at Different Distances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most portables are rated at 23 feet, often at a light load, while some built-in sets are listed at 10 feet. The numbers are not directly comparable, and every generator is louder under an air conditioner load. Use ratings to compare similar machines, and check campground limits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Generator in a Truck Bed or Under the Awning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A truck bed amplifies noise and can trap exhaust near the camper, and running under an awning or inside a toy hauler garage can let carbon monoxide into the RV. Set the generator on the ground, exhaust pointed away, and keep the CO alarm working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Generator Without the Right RV Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some compact inverters have only 20-amp outlets or a twist-lock rather than a TT-30R, so they need an adapter. That works, but check before you leave home. For a 30-amp RV, a generator with a built-in TT-30R outlet is the simplest connection.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

