        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for a 30 amp RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most 30-amp trailers are well served by a generator with about 3,000 to 3,700 running watts and a TT-30R outlet, which can start and run one 13,500 BTU air conditioner plus the fridge and lights. A generator around 2,000 to 2,800 rated watts can work if the air conditioner has a soft-start device.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for a 50 amp RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 50-amp RV can draw up to 12,000 watts, but most owners need around 6,000 to 8,000 running watts to run two air conditioners started one at a time. The options are a large inverter with a 14-50R outlet, such as the Champion 8500 dual fuel, or two inverters linked with a parallel kit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 2000 watt generator run an RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not on its own, because the compressor's starting surge exceeds what a typical 2,000-watt inverter can supply. Exceptions are generators built for it, such as Yamaha's EF2400iSHC, which Yamaha says starts most 13,500 BTU units, or a standard 2,000-watt unit paired with a soft-start device.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest RV generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among our picks, the Westinghouse iGen4500 has one of the lowest listed ratings, at 52 dBA, and the Yamaha EF2400iSHC is listed at 53 to 60 dBA. Ratings are measured at light loads and set distances, so every generator is louder when running an air conditioner. Placement and distance matter as much as the rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual fuel generator good for an RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for many RVers. You already carry propane, it stores without going stale, and it means carrying one fuel instead of two. The trade-off is slightly less power on propane; Champion lists its 4500 dual fuel at about 3,150 running watts on propane against 3,500 on gasoline.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my 50 amp RV into a 30 amp generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a 50-to-30-amp adapter, but the RV will only have about 3,600 watts available. That is enough for one air conditioner and light loads, not for two air conditioners. For full use of a 50-amp RV, you need a generator or parallel pair with a 50-amp outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a spark arrestor on my RV generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many public lands, including national forests, require one. Most inverter generators sold for RV use include a spark arrestor, but check before camping on public land, especially in dry seasons, and keep the screen clean so it does not restrict the engine.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

